<h2 id="bg-dat-01">BG-DAT-01: Start with a household question</h2>

**Local requirement: MUST.** Applies to dashboard and publication data presentation.

Write down what the person needs to know before you choose a graphic. For example: what is using power now, how usage changed, which device is unavailable, or where a sensor is. A frequently refreshed overview suits monitoring. A detailed explanation may need a report or a few related charts.

Show the main observation without making people click to find it. Do not write a reassuring message when readings are missing. A neutral title such as “Power sources” is more honest than a fixed claim that consumption is falling.


<h2 id="bg-dat-02">BG-DAT-02: Choose a chart type for the data relationship</h2>

**Local requirement: MUST.** Applies to chart type choice and maintained Energy chart scope.

Use lines for a time series, columns for interval quantities and horizontal bars to compare devices. Keep power (kW) and energy (kWh) apart. Use a map only when location answers the question. A pictorial percentage in a release graphic is not a template for a live consumption chart.

The maintained Energy adapter draws each Power series as its own unfilled, unsmoothed line and draws consumption and device bars with opaque fills. It keeps valid native bar stacks. Other chart families stay native unless the adapter contract lists them. Do not replace every chart with one decorative shape for consistency.


<h2 id="bg-dat-03">BG-DAT-03: Give each chart enough context</h2>

**Local requirement: MUST.** Applies to operational charts, static charts and exported data.

Give each chart a meaningful title, the measure and unit, the selected period and the source. In Home Assistant, the card title, native axis, date selector and device labels can supply these together, so do not repeat every label inside the plot. Give exported data its own caption, because the dashboard controls do not travel with it.

Say in words when a value is a forecast, when it compares another period, when exports are shown as negative values, or when data is missing. Put a short annotation next to its point and repeat essential context in text or the table. Do not add fixed annotations that claim something the integration has not supplied.


<h2 id="bg-dat-04">BG-DAT-04: Protect the underlying observations</h2>

**Local requirement: MUST.** Applies to native chart adaptation, data alternatives and lifecycle restoration.

Keep native values, IDs, timestamps, units, null values and controls as Home Assistant supplies them. Show a missing reading as a gap, not as zero. Do not smooth away a peak, force a line scale to start at zero, or use a thick plot edge to suggest a zero baseline that is outside the view. Comparisons keep their original observation dates.

The Power adapter’s signed projection picks the existing signed sample from verified pairs of native series. It does not calculate a new reading, and it leaves ambiguous pairs unchanged. A data table and its plot must cover the same rendered period, including while native updates arrive. When the adapter is removed, restore the native chart without bringing back stale observations.


<h2 id="bg-dat-05">BG-DAT-05: Use the operational chart specification</h2>

**Local requirement: MUST.** Applies to supported operational Energy plots, legends and tooltip markers.

Use the shared [chart-colour generator](chart-colour.html), legend keys that match the line or pattern, opaque paints and a **1.5px** line width in every state. Hover must not thicken a line or fade the other series.

A brand change leaves each source’s colour unchanged. Vivid or Muted and Light or Dark use the generator’s declared variants. A comparison keeps its source colour and adds hatching. Hatching, markers and functional strokes are deliberate cues that do not rely on colour, so the [illustration](illustration.html) limits on texture and outline do not apply to them. Infographic accents and other palettes do not replace this contract.


<h2 id="bg-dat-06">BG-DAT-06: Retain useful native interaction</h2>

**Local requirement: MUST.** Applies to maintained native Energy adapter and existing chart interaction.

Keep the native controls that answer repeated household questions: date changes, source visibility and device comparison. Preserve native keyboard controls, Chart2Music, zoom, legends and tooltips where Home Assistant supplies them. Explain any interaction that is unfamiliar. Do not replace a working native chart with an image that people cannot operate.

Home Assistant already owns ECharts and the chart data lifecycle, so civica does not add a second chart renderer. The maintained adapter uses the public ECharts calls `getOption` and `setOption`. It also depends on a private, version-sensitive host contract, described in [Compatibility and limits](../get-started/compatibility.html). Check that contract against the supported frontend version.


<h2 id="bg-dat-07">BG-DAT-07: Provide a usable alternative and honest scope</h2>

**Local requirement: MUST.** Applies to supported Energy data tables and proposed chart alternatives.

Use **View data** for supported Energy series. Show source names, original dates, units and signed values. Keep the native chart’s accessible name and keyboard interface. Say that the table holds the plotted values for the selected period, including hidden series, and is not a complete meter export. A missing interval cannot be rebuilt from a point the native chart left out.

Give every new chart or static report a matching table or a clear text summary. Label example data as invented. A table alone does not show that the chart’s colour, focus order or interaction work, so test the visual chart and the alternative separately.


<h2 id="bg-dat-08">BG-DAT-08: Treat maps as operational evidence</h2>

**Local requirement: MUST.** Applies to native map content and future spatial adapters.

Keep place labels, source attribution, observation time and a suitable extent. Give each marker a label or a distinct shape as well as colour. Keep a scale when distance matters. Add a text list of locations and statuses for people who cannot use the map.

Do not recolour tiles, cover a weather or flood layer, or move a position marker to fit a brand layout. Keep legend thresholds and source warnings. Label a floor plan as a floor plan, not a map, and label invented diagrams. These rules guide future map work. The Energy adapter does not restyle native map providers.


<h2 id="bg-dat-09">BG-DAT-09: Separate publishing graphics from live monitoring</h2>

**Local requirement: MUST.** Applies to publishing summaries versus operational monitoring.

A release graphic may sum up one fixed, sourced result with a simple shape or a large number. State its date, denominator, rounding and source, and give the facts as real text. Do not use area, size or motion to exaggerate the result. Say beside the graphic when an example is invented.

Operational dashboards need comparisons that repeat reliably and an accurate current state. Do not carry campaign gradients, oversized strokes, celebratory colours or unsupported claims into them. Review charts at narrow and desktop widths, with long labels and many devices. Verify the maintained adapter on the live route before you claim it is deployed.


## Review and evidence

The rule inventory and checklist are `docs/data/brand-guidance-inventory.json` and `docs/data/brand-guidance-checklist.json`. Reviewing a source is not a pass for an interface. Before you close a checklist item, record the page or component, theme and mode, test result and any remaining issue. Recheck after a palette, component or renderer change.
